  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Employer&apos;s Confederation of Thailand (ECOT) launched a comprehensive analysis on the current labour law and developed policy proposals for labour law revision to respond to the challenges brought by technological transformations and the COVID-19 pandemic. 
In doing so, ECOT conducted a survey of 200 enterprise and 15 qualitative interviews during November 2020 and March 2021 to use as a basis to form ECOT&apos;s positions on the enabling legal framework and environment that promote innovation, facilitate adaptation to the new demand towards competitive workforce and industries and respond to changes in the post-COVID working practices.
In July 2021, ECOT conducted a validation workshop with ECOT Board Members and 25 major member companies to gather input and views from employers on issues and challenges concerning labour law reform. Policy recommendations include the regulations on flexible management of working hours, annual leave, shutdown allowances, working from home and change of daily minimum wage to hourly wage. ECOT&apos;s policy proposal was developed in consultation with the research institute and ECOT Board Members. 
In September 2021, ECOT presented the policy recommendations on labour law revision as employer&apos;s key priorities at a meeting held for the national working group on bipartite social dialogue. 23 representatives of trade unions from Labour Congress of Thailand, National Congress Private Industrial of Employees, State Enterprises Workers&apos; Relations Confederation and Thai Trade Union Congress and 21 employer&apos;s representatives participated to promote consensus on the agenda for dialogue with the government. Workers and employers agreed to adopt the agenda to bring the labour law in line with future jobs and the COVID-19 pandemic. ECOT submitted the labour law revision policy proposal to the Vice-Minister of Labour at an ECOT workshop, entitled &quot;Labour market transitions during and post the COVID-19 pandemic&quot; on 26-27 November 2021. The ILO supported ECOT to review the laws and other regulations of Thailand related to technology and digital penetration. 
The ILO helped define the research scope and built capacity of the ECOT to join the research project and develop policy positions in partnership with the Chulongkorn University. Suggestions were made to make improvements to labour law to bring it in line with future jobs and the COVID-19 pandemic and to ensure appropriate response to the changing business environment and address employer concerns and questions.              
The ILO provided advice to ECOT on the design of survey/interview questions to gather employer input and views on issues and challenges concerning labour law reform. 
ILO supported ECOT to establish the national working group for bipartite social dialogue between workers and employers organizations through which the policy proposals can be submitted jointly to the Ministry of Labour. The ILO is supporting the background information and logistics for the working group meetings which are being held periodically - two times a year.</narrative>
   </description>
